00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.659
this page. and the URL is simply the base

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:58.229
NCBI homepage URL, forward slash, orffinder,

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:04.049
all lowercase. so what's new in ORFfinder
now? primarily it's the interface.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:08.429
it looks more like many of our other
resources, you'll see that particularly

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:13.980
on the results page, but we do now also
have a standalone version. you can

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.420
download that binary

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.989
here at this linux x64 link. and the
standalone version does not have the

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:27.780
limitation of processing 50 kb that the
web version does. the other addition is

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:33.390
you can now send your translated orf
directly to SmartBLAST, and we still

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.140
maintain the ability to send it to the
full blastp service as well.

00:02:37.709 --> 00:02:44.790
just a quick caveat before I demonstrate.
ORFfinder is not intended to be, you know,

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:50.010
a gene prediction tool, particularly for
eukaryotes. it works in many cases fine

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:56.220
as an ORFfinder in bacterial and
archaeal genomes, viral genomes and also

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.380
for eukaryotic transcripts.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.530
I'm not going to demonstrate the
transcripts today but there's a link

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.739
here to demonstrate that for you if you
want to look at that.

